<ProjectLayout slug="arkitera" styles={[beforeAfter, browser, diagram, page]}>
  <div class="cover">
    <figure class="cv-img" style="aspect-ratio: 16 / 9">
      <img
        src="/media/arkitera/cover.webp"
        alt="The redesigned Arkitera homepage on a laptop: the project of the week and the latest news"
      />
    </figure>
    <div class="cv-deco" aria-hidden="true">
      <span class="cv-ring"></span>
      <svg class="cv-ln" viewBox="0 0 106 30">
        <polyline class="cl" points="4.3,1.2 105.4,29.5"></polyline>
      </svg>
      <span class="x cv-x1"></span>
      <span class="x cv-x2"></span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="sec first" id="s-overview" data-sec aria-labelledby="h-overview">
    <div class="sh">
      <h2 class="sh-t" id="h-overview">
        Overview
      </h2>
      <span class="sh-l" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <span class="x sh-x" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </div>
    <div class="split">
      <div class="split-a">
        <p class="lead">Less a visual makeover than a UX one.</p>
        <p class="tx">
          Arkitera is Türkiye’s architecture publication. Its busy, flat homepage was reorganized into an open,
          hierarchical layout, the raw yellow-green was replaced with calm neutrals, and every label on the page now has
          a single job.
        </p>
        <p class="tx">
          Most changes are structural: what goes where, what is read first, and what is clickable. Visual polish follows
          from that, not the other way round. Nothing was removed from the site, only moved.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-b">
        <span class="vl" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <dl class="facts">
          <div>
            <dt>Publication</dt>
            <dd>Arkitera, Türkiye’s architecture publication</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Scope</dt>
            <dd>Homepage, badge system, light rebrand</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Year</dt>
            <dd>2026</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Status</dt>
            <dd>In progress</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="notes4">
      <div class="rv">
        <h4>UX before decoration</h4>
        <p>What goes where, what is read first and what is clickable come first. Visual polish follows from that.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rv">
        <h4>Hierarchy over density</h4>
        <p>
          One clear entry point, then sections in the order readers come for them. Fewer things compete for attention at
          the same level.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rv">
        <h4>Group by intent</h4>
        <p>
          Content that used to be interleaved in one long feed now lives in its own section, each with a single “see
          all” path.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rv">
        <h4>Calm, open layout</h4>
        <p>
          Neutral surfaces and generous spacing let architecture photography lead. Color is kept for meaning: type,
          status, action.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" id="s-home" data-sec aria-labelledby="h-home">
    <div class="sh">
      <h2 class="sh-t" id="h-home">
        Homepage
      </h2>
      <span class="sh-l" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <span class="x sh-x" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </div>
    <div class="stack">
      <p class="lead">The new homepage, top to bottom.</p>
      <p class="tx">
        A hero project with the five latest items beside it, then every open competition deadline, new projects, news
        and events, opinion and interviews, jobs and product promotions, and a dedicated place for advertisers.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="br rv" style="margin-left: 0; margin-right: 0; margin-bottom: 0">
